  <!-- FAQPage schema — EXISTING 8 questions preserved + 8 new additive questions -->
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I put my laptop charger in checked luggage?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes! Standard laptop chargers, cords, and power bricks contain no internal battery cells and are 100% allowed in checked luggage as well as carry-on bags. Only portable power banks (external battery packs) are restricted to carry-on."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I put an Apple AirTag in checked luggage?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes! The FAA, TSA, EASA, Air France, Lufthansa, and Emirates all officially approve Apple AirTags and CR2032 button-cell luggage trackers inside checked baggage."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I bring melatonin on a plane into the UK or internationally?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Solid melatonin pills and gummies are fully allowed through TSA checkpoints. While melatonin is prescription-only inside the UK, UK Border Force and MHRA allow incoming international visitors to bring up to a 3-month personal supply."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I bring nicotine pouches (ZYN) to Singapore or on planes?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Nicotine pouches are allowed on domestic US flights and throughout Europe (like Ryanair) in carry-on and checked luggage. However, they are STRICTLY PROHIBITED in Singapore, with fines up to SGD $2,000 at Changi Airport."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I take a jump rope, dumbbells, or tent stakes on a plane?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Jump ropes are 100% permitted in carry-on and checked bags. Metal tent stakes are prohibited in carry-on bags and MUST go in checked luggage. Heavy dumbbells are best checked due to cabin bludgeon hazards."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I bring food, cup noodles, nuts (pistachios/almonds), and bananas on a plane?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Solid snacks (nuts, whole bananas, protein bars, dry instant noodles) are fully allowed through TSA. For international flights (like Australia or the UK), declare all agricultural items and food on arrival."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I pack portable chargers, power banks, and AA batteries?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Power banks and lithium portable chargers must stay in carry-on baggage only (under 100Wh). Standard dry alkaline AA and AAA batteries can travel in either carry-on or checked luggage."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Are beard trimmers, electric shavers, and hair straighteners allowed on planes?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes! Electric beard trimmers, hair clippers, cordless shavers, and corded hair straighteners are allowed in both carry-on and checked luggage across TSA, Emirates, British Airways, and European carriers."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the TSA 3-1-1 rule for liquids?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "The TSA 3-1-1 rule means: containers must be 3.4 oz (100ml) or less, packed in 1 clear quart-sized zip-top bag, with 1 bag allowed per passenger through security. This applies to all liquids, gels, creams, pastes, and aerosols in your carry-on bag. Full-size liquids can go in your checked bag."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What items are prohibited in carry-on luggage by TSA?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "TSA prohibits in carry-on bags: firearms and ammunition, explosives, flammable items over 3.4 oz, sharp objects with blades over 4 inches, power banks over 160Wh, self-defense sprays, and flammable aerosols over 3.4 oz. Checked bags have different rules — always verify with BringOnPlane's item checker."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I bring a vape, e-cigarette, or electronic cigarette on a plane?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Vapes and e-cigarettes must travel in your carry-on bag or on your person — they are strictly prohibited in checked luggage due to their lithium batteries. You cannot use them during the flight. Destination country rules vary: vapes are illegal in countries like Singapore, Thailand, and India."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Is sunscreen, lotion, or shampoo considered a liquid by TSA?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. TSA classifies sunscreen, lotions, shampoo, conditioner, body wash, gel, cream, paste, and aerosol sprays all as liquids under the 3-1-1 rule. Each container must be 3.4 oz (100ml) or less in carry-on bags. Shampoo bars and solid sunscreen sticks are NOT classified as liquids and have no size restriction."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I bring prescription medication and pills on a plane?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. TSA allows prescription pills, over-the-counter medications, vitamins, and supplements in both carry-on and checked luggage in any quantity. Liquid medications are exempt from the 3-1-1 rule when declared at security. Keep medications in original labeled containers for international travel."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I use this TSA checker for international flights?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "BringOnPlane covers TSA rules (US departure airports), FAA regulations, IATA standards, and notes airline-specific variations for Air France, Emirates, Ryanair, British Airways, Singapore Airlines, Lufthansa, American Airlines, Delta, and United. For customs at your destination country, always check that country's specific customs authority."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "What is the carry-on bag size limit?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Most US airlines (American, Delta, United) allow carry-on bags up to 22 x 14 x 9 inches (56 x 36 x 23 cm). International carriers like Ryanair allow 55 x 40 x 20 cm, and Emirates allows 55 x 38 x 20 cm. Always verify with your specific airline, as size limits vary."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does BringOnPlane show airport security rules for every airline?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "BringOnPlane covers TSA (US), FAA battery rules, IATA liquid standards, and flags airline-specific variations for major global carriers. The tool is the fastest free way to check if any item is allowed in your carry-on or checked bag before you fly."
        }
      }
    ]
  })} />

